  "notes": "The most producer-forward records in the Cash Money catalogue — Mannie in front of the mic as well as behind the desk — and the slowest, brightest, most sung-at corner of this archetype. ⛔ **The gear conflict is the model's signature and it is directly sourced.** \"The old 808 midi does not work with the SP12 so we had to match the tempos\": two machines that would not sync, matched by hand. That is a humanize claim, not a tempo claim, so `quantizeStrength` drops to 0.81 — the loosest anywhere in this family — and every `timingJitterMs` lane rises. A Big Tymers generation should sit fractionally behind itself; that is period-correct rather than sloppy. ⛔ **`bassline.anticipationProb` drops to 0.08, inverting the parent's 0.22.** The research: the big 808 boom is \"placed to land *with* the hook's downbeat\". The parent's bass leans early into the bar; this one arrives on it. Paired with `transitions.dropOutBeats.prob` at 0.85, that is the drum-machine drop-out into the 808 hook entry the research names — the loudest arrangement gesture in this batch. ⛔ **Vocoder and synth brass**, so `timbreHint` is `synth_brass_stab` and `countermelody` runs `arp` first at a high `densityRatio`: a vocoder pad is a real second voice on these records, and the research names it as Mannie's starting point on the 1998 title track. ⚠ **Harmony is the one place this is more *stated* than the rest of the lane** — \"I–IV or I–V–vi motion under sung-ish hooks\" — so those two families lead, the single-chord family drops to the bottom, and `topVoiceStepwise` stays on because the top voice is supporting a sung line. ⚠ **Confidence `medium` because the research rates it high-medium**, and the 88–98 band is marked **[LOW-CONF]**. Inferred rather than sourced: the key list, every velocity and register bound, the `intervals`/`contour` split, `offSnare.prob` and the hi-hat weights. The gear, the tempo-matching workflow and the vocoder are the parts that are sourced. ⚠ `snareRoll.pitchWalk` is inherited.",
